# Feature Engineering Step 1: Interaction Features
# Feature name: Interaction_SpMax_L_J_Dz(e)
# Feature description: This feature represents the product of SpMax_L (leading eigenvalue from Laplace matrix) and J_Dz(e) (Balaban-like index from Barysz matrix). These two features are closely tied to molecular structure and electronegativity distribution, which are important determinants of biodegradability. Multiplying these features combines two complementary aspects of structural topology and electronegativity.
# Usefulness: Captures compound interaction between molecular structure and electronegativity behavior, enabling the model to better differentiate between biodegradable and non-biodegradable chemicals.
df['Interaction_SpMax_L_J_Dz(e)'] = df['SpMax_L'] * df['J_Dz(e)']
end

python
# Feature Engineering Step 2: Reciprocals for sparse features
# Feature name: Reciprocal_nCb-
# Feature description: Computes the reciprocal of the nCb- feature (number of substituted benzene C(sp2)), which may help mitigate the disproportionate effect of compounds with very high values. The sparsity in features (many zeros) and the high variability in this column's values warrant a feature transformation to compress extreme values.
# Usefulness: This transformation accounts for the skewness in the data and reduces the impact of outliers while retaining meaningful information about molecular substitution patterns.
df['Reciprocal_nCb-'] = df['nCb-'].replace(0, np.nan).apply(lambda x: 1/x if pd.notnull(x) else 0)
end

python
# Feature Engineering Step 3: Z-Score Normalization for Scale-sensitive Features
# Feature name: ZScore_SdO
# Feature description: Calculates the z-score of the SdO feature (sum of dO E-states), which has a range from [0.0, 71.167] and a high standard deviation. Normalizing this attribute will ensure its values are not excessively weighted due to its natural large-scale behavior compared to other features.
# Usefulness: Normalizing this feature prepares it for machine learning algorithms, especially those sensitive to differences in feature magnitude, improving downstream model predictions.
df['ZScore_SdO'] = (df['SdO'] - df['SdO'].mean()) / df['SdO'].std()
end

python
# Feature Dropping: Remove highly sparse and redundant features
# Explanation why the column F01[N-N] is dropped
df.drop(columns=['F01[N-N]'], inplace=True)
# Reason: F01[N-N] has extremely low frequency (Mean = 0.0458, 75% of values = 0.0) and does not carry sufficient distinguishing information for biodegradability. This sparsity greatly limits its utility in downstream predictions.

# Explanation why the column B01[C-Br] is dropped
df.drop(columns=['B01[C-Br]'], inplace=True)
# Reason: B01[C-Br] is dominated by zero values (Mean = 0.0395, Std = 0.1949) indicating few relevant compounds feature bromine bonding. Redundant data with negligible contribution to predictions.

# Explanation why the column N-073 is dropped
df.drop(columns=['N-073'], inplace=True)
# Reason: Similar to F01[N-N], N-073 has extremely low variability (Mean = 0.0395, Median = 0.0) and does not improve biodegradability classification, per exploratory data analysis.
end

