oxygen atoms relative to carbon, which could correlate with increased or decreased biodegradability.\ndf['nO_x_O_to_C_ratio'] = df['nO'] * df['O_to_C_ratio']\nend\n\npython\n# Step 2: Log transformation of SpMax_A_SdO_interaction\n# Feature name: Log_SpMax_A_SdO\n# Feature description: Natural logarithm of SpMax_A_SdO_interaction, a numerical feature with a large range and positive skewness.\n# Usefulness: Log transformation can stabilize the variance, reduce skewness, and make the feature's distribution more Gaussian, which may improve model performance.\ndf['Log_SpMax_A_SdO'] = df['SpMax_A_SdO_interaction'].apply(lambda x: np.log(x + 1))  # Add +1 to avoid log(0)\nend\n\npython\n# Step 3: Ratio of nHM (Number of heavy atoms) to nO (Number of oxygen atoms)  \n# Feature name: HM_to_O_ratio\n# Feature description: Compute the ratio of heavy atoms to oxygen atoms in the chemical structure.\n# Usefulness: This feature may help identify structural patterns related to biodegradability, especially how oxygen presence relates to a molecule's complexity.\n# Avoid division by zero error by adding a small constant to the denominator\ndf['HM_to_O_ratio'] = df['nHM'] / (df['nO'] + 1e-8)\nend\n\npython\n# Dropping a redundant column\n# Explanation why the column O_to_C_ratio is dropped\n# O_to_C_ratio has been used to create the feature nO_x_O_to_C_ratio, so the original column may now be redundant.\ndf.drop(columns=['O_to_C_ratio'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Dropping a high cardinality or low-information column\n# Explanation why the column F01[N-N] is dropped\n# The feature F01[N-N] has very low variability (Mean = 0.0458, Std = 0.2688), with most values being 0.\n# This suggests it is largely uninformative, so removing it could reduce noise and improve the model's ability to generalize.\ndf.drop(columns=['F01[N-N]'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Dropping a redundant column\n# Explanation why the column nCRX3 is dropped\n# The feature nCRX3 has very low variability (Mean = 0.0253, Std = 0.1932), and nearly all values are 0.\n# This indicates that it likely doesn't contribute significantly to the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['nCRX3'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nSummary:  \n1. First step creates a meaningful interaction between oxygen-related features (nO and O_to_C_ratio) to capture additional relationships impacting biodegradability.  \n2. Second step applies a log transformation on a highly skewed feature to improve modeling efficacy.  \n3. Third step generates a useful ratio HM_to_O_ratio that describes the relationship between heavy atoms and oxygen presence, which may directly relate to the target.  \nFinally, dropping low-information or redundant columns helps prevent potential noise and improves the model's focus on critical and relevant features.", "python\n# Step 1: Ratio of oxygen atoms to heavy atoms (nO/nHM)\n# Feature description: This feature calculates the proportion of oxygen atoms (nO) relative to the number of heavy atoms (nHM). This can provide insights into the oxygen density in a molecule, which may affect its biodegradability.\n# Usefulness: Since oxygen atoms are often involved in chemical processes that affect biodegradability (e.g., acting as electron acceptors in redox reactions), this ratio can be a critical feature in predicting the class of chemicals.\ndf['nO_to_nHM_ratio'] = df['nO'] / (df['nHM'] + 1e-6)  # Adding a small constant to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Step 2: Log-transformed SpMax_A_SdO_interaction\n# Feature description: The log transformation of SpMax_A_SdO_interaction makes the distribution less skewed, enhancing the signal-to-noise ratio in downstream models for heavily right-skewed features.\n# Usefulness: SpMax_A_SdO_interaction has a wide range [0.0, 175.213]. Log transformation helps mitigate large outliers and captures relative differences better, improving downstream model sensitivities for this feature.\ndf['Log_SpMax_A_SdO_interaction'] = np.log(df['SpMax_A_SdO_interaction']