lectronic states of oxygen atoms. Their interaction might synergistically highlight structural motifs that influence biodegradability (e.g., highly connected oxygen atoms playing key roles in reactivity).\n\ndf['SpMax_A_SdO_interaction'] = df['SpMax_A'] * df['SdO']\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Psi_i_1d' is dropped\n# 'Psi_i_1d' (Intrinsic state pseudoconnectivity index - type 1d) has a very narrow range and almost universally hovers around 0.0 (Mean = -0.0012, Std = 0.1626). \n# This lack of variability means the feature is unlikely to provide useful discriminatory power or contribute meaningfully to the model's performance.\ndf.drop(columns=['Psi_i_1d'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'B01[C-Br]' is dropped \n# 'B01[C-Br]' represents the presence of C-Br bonds at topological distance 1 but appears to be a very sparse feature (Mean = 0.0395, Median = 0.0).\n# A sparse column with very few non-zero values may increase model noise without enhancing discriminatory power, especially given that brominated compounds are rare in the dataset.\ndf.drop(columns=['B01[C-Br]'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'N-073' is dropped\n# 'N-073' represents specific nitrogen-containing functional groups but has negligible variance (Mean = 0.0395, Median = 0.0, Q3 = 0.0), indicating it has limited variability across the dataset.\n# With such low frequency, this column is unlikely to contribute significantly to the model while adding computational overhead.\ndf.drop(columns=['N-073'], inplace=True)\nend", "python\n# Step 1: Interaction between nO and O_to_C_ratio\n# Feature name: nO_x_O_to_C_ratio\n# Feature description: Interaction term created by multiplying nO (number of oxygen atoms) with O_to_C_ratio. \n# This feature captures the combined effect of oxygen atoms and their ratio to carbon atoms, which may influence biodegradability.\n# Usefulness: This combination may highlight compounds with a higher density of oxygen atoms relative to carbon, which could correlate with increased or decreased biodegradability.\ndf['nO_x_O_to_C_ratio'] = df['nO'] * df['O_to_C_ratio']\nend\n\npython\n# Step 2: Log transformation of SpMax_A_SdO_interaction\n# Feature name: Log_SpMax_A_SdO\n# Feature description: Natural logarithm of SpMax_A_SdO_interaction, a numerical feature with a large range and positive skewness.\n# Usefulness: Log transformation can stabilize the variance, reduce skewness, and make the feature's distribution more Gaussian, which may improve model performance.\ndf['Log_SpMax_A_SdO'] = df['SpMax_A_SdO_interaction'].apply(lambda x: np.log(x + 1))  # Add +1 to avoid log(0)\nend\n\npython\n# Step 3: Ratio of nHM (Number of heavy atoms) to nO (Number of oxygen atoms)  \n# Feature name: HM_to_O_ratio\n# Feature description: Compute the ratio of heavy atoms to oxygen atoms in the chemical structure.\n# Usefulness: This feature may help identify structural patterns related to biodegradability, especially how oxygen presence relates to a molecule's complexity.\n# Avoid division by zero error by adding a small constant to the denominator\ndf['HM_to_O_ratio'] = df['nHM'] / (df['nO'] + 1e-8)\nend\n\npython\n# Dropping a redundant column\n# Explanation why the column O_to_C_ratio is dropped\n# O_to_C_ratio has been used to create the feature nO_x_O_to_C_ratio, so the original column may now be redundant.\ndf.drop(columns=['O_to_C_ratio'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Dropping a high cardinality or low-information column\n# Explanation why the column F01[N-N] is dropped\n# The feature F01[N-N] has very low variability (Mean = 0.0458, Std = 0.2688), with most values being 0.\n# This suggests it is largely uninformative, so removing it could reduce noise and improve the model's ability to generalize.\ndf.drop(columns=['F01[N-N]'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Dropping a redundant column\n# Explanation why the column nCRX3 is dropped\n# The feature nCRX3 has very low variability (Mean = 0.0253, Std = 0.1932), and nearly all values are 0.\n# This indicates that it likely doesn't contribute significantly to the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['nCRX3'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nSummary:  \n1. First step creates a meaningful interaction between oxygen-related features (nO and O_to_C_ratio) to capture additional relationships impacting biodegradability.  \n2. Second step applies a log transformation on a highly skewed feature to improve modeling efficacy.  \n3. Third step generates a useful ratio HM_to_O_ratio that describes the relationship between heavy atoms and oxygen presence, which may directly relate to the target.  \nFinally, dropping low-information or redundant columns helps prevent potential noise and improves the model's focus on critical and relevant features.", "python\n# Step 1: Ratio of oxygen atoms to heavy atoms (nO/nHM)\n# Feature description: This feature calculates the proportion of oxygen atoms (nO) relative to the number of heavy atoms (nHM). This can provide insights into the oxygen density in a molecule, which may affect its biodegradability.\n# Usefulness: Since oxygen atoms are often involved in chemical processes that affect biodegradability (e.g., acting as electron acceptors in redox reactions), this ratio can be a critical feature in predicting the class of chemicals.\ndf['nO_to_nHM_ratio'] = df['nO'] / (df['nHM'] + 1e-6)  # Adding a small constant to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Step 2: Log-transformed SpMax_A_SdO_interaction\n# Feature description: The log transformation of SpMax_A_SdO_interaction makes the distribution less skewed, enhancing the signal-to-noise ratio in downstream models for heavily right-skewed features.\n# Usefulness: SpMax_A_SdO_interaction has a wide range [0.0, 175.213]. Log transformation helps mitigate large outliers and captures relative differences better, improving downstream model sensitivities for this feature.\ndf['Log_SpMax_A_SdO_interaction'] = np.log(df['SpMax_A_SdO_interaction'] + 1e-6)  # Adding small constant to avoid log(0)\nend\n\npython\n# Step 3: Interaction feature between SpMax_B(m) and number of donor atoms for H-bonds (nHDon)\n# Feature description: This feature multiplies SpMax_B(m) (a structural descriptor) with nHDon to capture the combined effect of molecular size/shape (SpMax_B(m)) and potential hydrogen bonding (nHDon).\n# Usefulness: Molecules with high SpMax_B(m) and high H-bond potential may exhibit specific behavior related to solubility or biodegradability, making this interaction important in identifying complex patterns.\ndf['SpMax_B_m_x_nHDon'] = df['SpMax_B(m)'] * df['nHDon']\nend\n\npython\n# Drop Column: HM_to_O_ratio\n# Explanation why the column HM_to_O_ratio is dropped: The feature range [0.0, 1.2e9] is highly unbalanced and contains extreme outliers (e.g., large ratio values like 1.2e9). Additionally, its non-logarithmic nature limits its interpretability and signal-to-noise ratio for downstream models. A more meaningful version of this feature (e.g., a capped or log-transformed ratio) can be engineered separately if necessary.\ndf.drop(columns=['HM_to_O_ratio'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Drop Column: nO_x_O_to_C_ratio\n# Explanation why the column nO_x_O_to_C_ratio is dropped: Although it potentially captures oxygen-to-carbon relationships, its usefulness is unclear given the low median value (0.059) and disproportionately large feature range [0.0, 4.22]. Other features like nO_to_nHM_ratio or SdO already overlap with oxygen-related descriptors, making it redundant.\ndf.drop(columns=['nO_x_O_to_C_ratio'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Step 4 (Optional): Grouping categorical or binary features for median aggregation\n# Feature description: Aggregate binary features related to topological distances (e.g., F02[C-N], F03[C-O], F03[C-N], F04[C-N]) by calculating a topology sum.\n# Usefulness: Topological frequencies at different distances contain valuable but fragmented information. Summing across these distances can compress the information in a meaningful way to summarize connectivity patterns.\ndf['topological_frequency_sum'] = df[['F02[C-N]', 'F03[C-O]', 'F03[C-N]', 'F04[C-N]']].sum(axis=1)\nend", "python\n# Step 1: Interaction Term - Multiply 'nO' (number of oxygen atoms) by 'nHDon' (number of donor atoms for H-bonds)\n# Feature name: nO_x_nHDon\n# Feature description: This feature captures the combined effect of oxygen atoms and hydrogen bond donors in the molecule. Oxygen atoms are essential contributors to biodegradability, and their ability to form hydrogen bonds significantly impacts solubility and chemical reactivity, affecting biodegradation.\n# Usefulness: Molecules with a high number of both attributes may have higher biodegradability due to increased interactions with water or enzymes, a crucial property for the classification task.\n\ndf['nO_x_nHDon'] = df['nO'] * df['nHDon']\nend\n\npython\n# Step 2: Normalized Ratio - nO to Total Heavy Atoms\n# Feature name: nO_to_TotalHeavyAtoms\n# Feature description: Compute the ratio of oxygen atoms (nO) to the sum of heavy atoms (nHM) and halogen atoms (nX). This provides insight into the concentration of oxygen atoms relative to the overall mass and structure of the molecule, which may influence biodegradability.\n# Usefulness: Oxygen density normalized by the total heavy and halogen atoms could highlight structural differences between ready biodegradable and not ready biodegradable chemicals.\n\ndf['nO_to_TotalHeavyAtoms'] = df['nO'] / (df['nHM'] + df['nX'] + 1e-6)  # Adding a small value to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Step 3: Log Transformation of 'topological_frequency_sum'\n# Feature name: Log_topological_frequency_sum\n# Feature description: Apply a natural log transformation to 'topological_frequency_sum' to reduce the skewness of the distribution. Topological frequency-related features aggregate information about the molecular structure, and a normalized distribution helps machine learning models capture patterns more effectively.\n# Usefulness: Log transformations are particularly valuable for reducing the effect of outliers and improving model performance by stabilizing variance in highly skewed features.\n\nimport numpy as np\ndf['Log_topological_frequency_sum'] = np.log1p(df['topological_frequency_sum'])  # Use log1p to handle potential zero values gracefully\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Log_SpMax_A_SdO' is dropped\n# 'Log_SpMax_A_SdO' is dropped because of high correlation with the column 'Log_SpMax_A_SdO_interaction'. Both features are derived from 'SpMax_A_SdO_interaction', and retaining both may introduce multicollinearity, negatively impacting model performance.\ndf.drop(columns=['Log_SpMax_A_SdO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'SpMax_B_m_x_nHDon' is dropped\n# 'SpMax_B_m_x_nHDon' may be removed because, although it captures an interaction between structural features and hydrogen bond donors, the dataset already includes other interaction terms and structural descriptors with better statistical properties (e.g., fewer missing values or lower skewness).\ndf.drop(columns=['SpMax_B_m_x_nHDon'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'nO_to_nHM_ratio' is dropped\n# The 'nO_to_nHM_ratio' feature is redundant because 'nO_to_TotalHeavyAtoms' is a more comprehensive feature, factoring in both heavy atoms (nHM) and halogens (nX). Retaining both features may result in redundant information and unnecessarily inflate feature space.\ndf.drop(columns=['nO_to_nHM_ratio'], inplace=True)\nend"), with improvements -
